//! `Section`: a process's critical section against its own threads (spec §7).
//!
//! The section word is the runtime's (`sosrt`), one per process, and the
//! kernel learns where it is from `Process.section_register()`. Entering and
//! leaving are the runtime's adds on it, so this file holds no state and makes
//! no trap of its own.

import sosrt.{section_enter, section_leave, section_pending}

/// The calling process's critical section: while a thread of the process is
/// inside it, no other thread of the process runs, and threads of other
/// processes run as they would.
///
/// It is the ceiling a process takes against its own threads, for state they
/// share that is short to touch and is touched by threads at several levels:
/// a sibling that would have run is held back without being run, and the
/// thread inside runs in its place until it leaves, so a higher sibling never
/// waits behind a lower thread that a third thread took the processor from.
/// Nothing traps to enter or leave, but the leave that lets a held-back sibling
/// run.
///
/// Nothing inside may wait. A thread that parks inside the section (a `wait`,
/// a `join`, a call, a print through a console driver whose ring is full), or
/// exits inside it, ends its process, because no sibling could run again.
///
/// A print inside the section is safe only while no sibling holds the
/// runtime's output lock. A print waits for that lock by yielding, and the
/// held section gives the processor straight back to the waiting holder, so
/// a print behind a sibling that holds the lock waits for good. With the
/// lock free, a print through the kernel's debug print never parks.
///
/// The section word must be registered first (`Process.section_register()`):
/// entering one the kernel was never given panics, since it would hold
/// nothing back.
public struct Section {}

extension Section {
    /// Enter the section, or enter it once more: sections nest, and the
    /// section is left at the last `leave`.
    public static func enter() {
        section_enter()
    }

    /// Leave the section once. The last leave gives the processor up when
    /// the section held a thread back, so that thread runs now. A thread
    /// with no Thread handle of its own where the runtime keeps one (one a
    /// `main` program or `thread_create(run:)` did not make) cannot give the
    /// processor up, so its leave does not, and the thread it held back runs
    /// at the next switch. Leaving a section that is not held panics.
    public static func leave() {
        section_leave()
    }

    /// Whether the section held a thread back since the kernel last found it
    /// left: what the last `leave` reads to decide whether to give the
    /// processor up.
    public static func pending() -> Bool {
        section_pending()
    }
}
